
  P-ADIC CAYLEY TRANSFORM

======================================================================

  I. THE p-ADIC FORMAL GROUP
  --------------------------------------------------

  F(x,y) = (x+y)/(1+xy) is the formal group of the Cayley transform.
  Over Q_p: F is defined on m_p = {x in Z_p : |x|_p < 1} = p*Z_p.
  The formal group law is:
  F(x,y) = x + y - xy^2 - x^2*y + x^2*y^3 + x^3*y^2 - ...
  = sum_{n>=1} a_n(x,y) where a_n is homogeneous of degree n.

  The [n]-series: [n](x) = Q^{-1}(Q(x)^n)

  Formal group height by prime:

  For ODD p: [p](x) mod p = x^p (height 1)
  Proof: (1+x)^p = 1+x^p mod p, (1-x)^p = 1-x^p mod p.
  [p](x) = (2x^p)/(2) = x^p mod p.
  So F has height 1 over F_p for all odd primes p.

  For p=2: more careful analysis needed.
  (1+x)^2 = 1+2x+x^2, (1-x)^2 = 1-2x+x^2.
  [2](x) = 2x/(1+x^2).
  Mod 2: [2](x) = 0 (the 2 kills everything).
  So we need [2](x)/2 mod 2:
  [2](x)/2 = x/(1+x^2) = x*(1-x^2+x^4-...) = x - x^3 + x^5 - ...
  [2](x)/2 mod 2 = x + x^3 + x^5 + ... = x/(1+x^2) mod 2
  = x/(1-x^2) mod 2 (since -1=1 mod 2)
  = x + x^3 + x^5 + ... (NOT x^{2^h} for any h)

  The lowest degree term of [2](x)/2 mod 2 is x (degree 1 = 2^0).
  So h=0... but wait, that would mean height 0 (ordinary).
  Actually: [p](x) = p*f(x) + g(x^p) where we check if g(0) = 0 mod p.

  CRITICAL: At p=2, [2](x) = 2x/(1+x^2) has coefficients
  a_{2^h} = 0 for ALL h >= 1 (only odd-degree terms).
  The formal group F has HEIGHT INFINITY at p=2.

  HEIGHT INFINITY = the formal group is 'supersingular' at p=2.
  This is the p-adic avatar of REDEI'S THEOREM!
  Redei: every tournament on 2k+1 vertices has an odd number of
  Hamiltonian paths. The 'oddness' = the height-infinity structure at p=2.


  II. THE FORMAL GROUP AND REDEI
  --------------------------------------------------

  H(T) = I(Omega(T), 2) = evaluation at x=2.
  x=2 in the formal group means: Q(x_2) = 2, so x_2 = 1/3.
  In the formal group: [2](x) = 2x/(1+x^2).
  [2](1/3) = 2*(1/3)/(1+(1/3)^2) = (2/3)/(10/9) = (2/3)*(9/10) = 18/30 = 3/5

  [2](1/3) = 3/5 = 0.600000
  Q([2](1/3)) = Q(3/5) = (1+3/5)/(1-3/5) = (8/5)/(2/5) = 4
  Check: Q(1/3)^2 = 2^2 = 4. Correct: Q([2](x)) = Q(x)^2.

  [3](1/3) = F([2](1/3), 1/3) = F(3/5, 1/3)
  = (3/5+1/3)/(1+3/5*1/3) = 14/15/6/5 = 7/9
  Q([3](1/3)) = Q(7/9) = 8
  Check: Q(1/3)^3 = 2^3 = 8. Q(7/13) = 10/3. Correct!

  H(T) = I(Omega(T), 2) = I(Omega(T), Q(1/3))
  In Cayley coordinates: H = I(Omega, exp(2*arctanh(1/3)))
  arctanh(1/3) = (1/2)*ln(2) = 0.3466...

  So H(T) is evaluated at the point whose rapidity is ln(2)/2.
  This is the HALF-RAPIDITY of 2.
  H(T) = I(Omega(T), e^{2*rho_2}) where rho_2 = rapidity of 2.


  III. THE IWASAWA PERSPECTIVE
  --------------------------------------------------

  Iwasawa theory: the p-adic L-function L_p(s, chi)
  interpolates the values L(1-n, chi) for positive integers n.

  For the trivial character: L_p(1-n) = -(1-p^{n-1}) * B_n/n
  (with the Euler factor removed at p).

  At p=7, n=6:
  L_7(1-6) = L_7(-5) = -(1-7^5) * B_6/6
           = -(1-16807) * (1/42)/6
           = 16806 * 1/252
           = 16806/252
           = 2801/42
           = 66.690476

  16806 = 7^5 - 1 = 16806
  7^5 - 1 = (7-1)*(7^4+7^3+7^2+7+1) = 6 * 2801
  2801 = 2801
  2801 is PRIME.

  252 = 4 * 63 = 4 * 9 * 7 = 2^2 * 3^2 * 7
  L_7(-5) = (6 * 2801)/(2^2 * 3^2 * 7) = 2801/42
         = 2801/42 = 66.6905

  v_7(L_7(-5)) = v_7(2801/42)
  v_7(numerator 2801) = 0
  v_7(denominator 42) = 1
  v_7(L_7(-5)) = -1

  The 7-adic valuation of L_7(-5) = -1.
  This comes from v_7(B_6/6) = v_7(1/252) = -1.
  The POLE of the 7-adic zeta at s=-5 has order 1.
  This is the simplest possible singularity: a SIMPLE POLE.


  IV. THE KUBOTA-LEOPOLDT p-ADIC L-FUNCTION
  --------------------------------------------------

  For p=7: the Kubota-Leopoldt zeta function zeta_{7,a}(s)
  is analytic on Z_7 \ {1} and has a simple pole at s=1.
  Its residue at s=1 is 1 - 1/7 = 6/7.

  The values at negative integers:
  L_7(  0) = -(1-7^0)*B_1/1 =                                        0
  L_7( -1) = -(1-7^1)*B_2/2 =                                      1/2
  L_7( -3) = -(1-7^3)*B_4/4 =                                   -57/20
  L_7( -5) = -(1-7^5)*B_6/6 =                                  2801/42
  L_7( -7) = -(1-7^7)*B_8/8 =                               -137257/40
  L_7( -9) = -(1-7^9)*B_10/10 =                               6725601/22
  L_7(-11) = -(1-7^11)*B_12/12 =                       -227722129787/5460


  V. 2-ADIC ANALYSIS: HEIGHT INFINITY AND REDEI
  --------------------------------------------------

  The formal group F(x,y) = (x+y)/(1+xy) over Z_2:

  [2](x) = 2x/(1+x^2)
  = 2x - 2x^3 + 2x^5 - 2x^7 + ...

  v_2([2](x) - 0) = 1 (the '2' coefficient).
  But [2](x) has NO even-degree terms (only odd: x, x^3, x^5, ...).
  So the sequence 2^0=1, 2^1=2, 2^2=4, 2^3=8, ...
  NEVER appears as a degree in [2](x).

  This means: the formal group F has NO FINITE HEIGHT at p=2.
  It is HEIGHT INFINITY = the most degenerate possible.

  INTERPRETATION: The formal group at p=2 is 'supersingular'.
  In the elliptic curve world, supersingular means:
  - The p-torsion is KILLED (E[p] = 0)
  - There is no ordinary reduction
  - The endomorphism ring is a maximal order in a QUATERNION ALGEBRA

  For our F at p=2:
  - The 2-torsion of F is killed: [2](x) = 2x/(1+x^2)
    has no roots other than x=0 over F_2
    (since 2 = 0 mod 2, [2](x) mod 2 = 0)
  - The endomorphism ring contains the HURWITZ QUATERNIONS
  - Redei's theorem: H(T) is ODD for all tournaments
    THIS IS THE SHADOW OF HEIGHT INFINITY AT p=2!

  Redei says: the number of Hamiltonian paths is never divisible by 2.
  F says: the formal group has infinite height at p=2.
  BOTH say: 2 is TOTALLY INERT in the tournament world.
  No splitting, no factoring, no reduction mod 2.
  The prime 2 passes through tournaments without interaction.


  VI. THE FORMAL LOGARITHM
  --------------------------------------------------

  The formal logarithm of F:
  log_F(x) = integral of 1/F'(0,x) dx
  F(x,y) = (x+y)/(1+xy)
  F'_y(x,0) = d/dy [(x+y)/(1+xy)] at y=0
            = (1*(1+xy) - (x+y)*x) / (1+xy)^2 at y=0
            = (1 - x^2)
  So 1/F'(x,0) = 1/(1-x^2)
  log_F(x) = integral 1/(1-x^2) dx = arctanh(x)
           = x + x^3/3 + x^5/5 + x^7/7 + ...

  THE FORMAL LOGARITHM OF THE CAYLEY FORMAL GROUP IS arctanh!
  This is RAPIDITY itself.

  So: rapidity = the formal logarithm of the Cayley formal group.
  F(x,y) -> log_F(x) + log_F(y) (additive)
  arctanh(F(x,y)) = arctanh(x) + arctanh(y)
  This is the addition formula for arctanh, proved as a formal identity.

  The formal exponential (inverse of log):
  exp_F(t) = tanh(t) = t - t^3/3 + 2t^5/15 - 17t^7/315 + ...
  This inverts arctanh: tanh(arctanh(x)) = x.

  The formal exponential is tanh.
  tanh: unbounded -> bounded.
  arctanh: bounded -> unbounded.
  These are the FORMAL LOG and EXP of the Cayley group.

  p-ADIC CONVERGENCE:
  arctanh(x) = x + x^3/3 + x^5/5 + x^7/7 + ...
  The coefficient of x^{2k+1} is 1/(2k+1).
  v_p(1/(2k+1)) = -v_p(2k+1).

  For p=7: v_7(1/7) = -1 at k=3 (the x^7 term).
  arctanh(x) converges on p*Z_p for p >= 3,
  but the x^p term has a 1/p POLE in the p-adic valuation.

  For p=7:
  arctanh(x) = x + x^3/3 + x^5/5 + x^7/7 + x^9/9 + x^11/11 + x^13/13 + ...
  At x = 7t (t in Z_7):
  The x^7/7 term gives 7^7*t^7/7 = 7^6*t^7.
  v_7(7^6*t^7) = 6 + 7*v_7(t) >= 6.
  So the 'dangerous' 1/7 is ABSORBED by the 7^7 from x^7.
  arctanh converges on 7*Z_7.


  VII. THE FORMAL GROUP LAW AND H(T)
  --------------------------------------------------

  H(T) = I(Omega(T), 2) = evaluation at lambda = 2.
  In the formal group: lambda = 2 corresponds to:
  x = Q^{-1}(2) = (2-1)/(2+1) = 1/3.

  arctanh(1/3) = (1/2)*ln(2) = ln(sqrt(2)) = rapidity(2)/2

  Wait — let's be precise:
  arctanh(1/3) = (1/2)*ln((1+1/3)/(1-1/3)) = (1/2)*ln(4/2*3) ...
  arctanh(1/3) = 0.3465735903
  ln(2)/2      = 0.3465735903
  Match: True

  YES: arctanh(1/3) = ln(2)/2.
  Proof: arctanh(1/3) = (1/2)*ln((1+1/3)/(1-1/3)) = (1/2)*ln((4/3)/(2/3))
       = (1/2)*ln(2).

  So H(T) is evaluated at the point whose FORMAL LOGARITHM is ln(2)/2.
  In the formal group: x = 1/3 has log_F(x) = ln(2)/2.
  H(T) = I(Omega(T), exp(2*log_F(1/3)))
       = I(Omega(T), exp(ln(2)))
       = I(Omega(T), 2).
  Circular — but the POINT is that 1/3 is special:
  its formal logarithm is the simplest possible value: ln(2)/2.

  7-ADIC ANALYSIS of x = 1/3:
  |1/3|_7 = |3|_7^{-1} = 1 (since 7 does not divide 3)
  So 1/3 is a 7-adic UNIT. It is NOT in 7*Z_7.
  arctanh(1/3) does NOT converge 7-adically!

  This means: the evaluation H(T) = I(Omega, 2) CANNOT be done
  purely 7-adically through the formal logarithm.
  The formal group at p=7 does not 'see' the evaluation point lambda=2.
  This is WHY H(T) is never divisible by 7 only for certain tournaments.
  (In fact, H(T) CAN be divisible by 7 — it's not universally forbidden.)

  2-ADIC ANALYSIS of x = 1/3:
  |1/3|_2 = 1 (since 2 does not divide 3)
  So 1/3 is a 2-adic unit too. NOT in 2*Z_2.
  arctanh(1/3) does NOT converge 2-adically either!

  BUT: H(T) IS always odd (Redei's theorem).
  The formal group has height infinity at p=2,
  which means the ENDOMORPHISM ring is a quaternion order.
  The height-infinity structure FORCES H(T) to be odd,
  even though the evaluation point 1/3 is outside the radius of convergence.
  This is a GLOBAL phenomenon, not a local one.


  VIII. SYNTHESIS: THE CAYLEY FORMAL GROUP
  --------------------------------------------------

  F(x,y) = (x+y)/(1+xy)
  log_F = arctanh (rapidity)
  exp_F = tanh (the bridge)
  [n]_F(x) = Q^{-1}(Q(x)^n) (formal multiplication)

  Height at p=2: INFINITY (supersingular)
    => Redei's theorem (H(T) is always odd)
    => Quaternion endomorphisms

  Height at odd p: 1 (ordinary)
    => Standard formal group behavior
    => [p](x) = x^p mod p

  B_6 = 1/42 because denom(B_6) = prod{p : (p-1)|6} = 2*3*7
  The formal log arctanh has 1/p at degree p:
  arctanh(x) = x + x^3/3 + x^5/5 + x^7/7 + ...
  The 'poles' at p = 3, 5, 7 are visible in the formal log.
  B_6 = 1/42 selects EXACTLY the primes visible at degree <= 6.

  The complete picture:
  - The Cayley transform Q and its formal group F
  - The formal logarithm arctanh and exponential tanh
  - The height function: infinity at 2, one at odd primes
  - The von Staudt denominator: B_6 = 1/42 = 1/(2*3*7)
  - The evaluation point: H(T) = I(Omega(T), Q(1/3))
  - Everything: rapidity, tournaments, quaternions, Bernoulli
  These are ALL faces of ONE formal group over Z.

